course: english
lesson: 28
title: Clauses: main, subordinate and joining words
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A clause is a group of words with a verb in it. A main clause makes sense on its own: «The dog barked.» A subordinate clause has a verb too, but it leans on a main clause and can't stand alone: «when the postman came». Jack is right that «When I got home» is not a full sentence: it is a subordinate clause waiting for its main clause. «When I got home, I ate my tea.»
explain: Subordinate clauses usually start with a subordinating conjunction: because, when, if, although, while, after, before, until, since or as. Isla writes «Because it was raining.» on its own, and it feels unfinished, like a door left open. Join it: «We stayed in because it was raining.» Aoife in Belfast asks about the wee (little) sentence «If you're quick, you'll catch the bus.» Which bit's the main? Cover each part: «you'll catch the bus» makes sense alone, so that is the main clause.
explain: The comma rule: when the subordinate clause comes first, put a comma after it. «Although I wasn't hungry, I ate my dinner.» When the main clause comes first, you usually don't need one: «I ate my dinner although I wasn't hungry.» Jacob says, «Bro, "while I was gaming, Mum called", that's a clause, no cap» (no cap means no lie). He's right: «while I was gaming» is a subordinate clause and «Mum called» is the main clause.
explain: A phrase has no verb. «After the match» is a phrase; «after the match ended» is a clause, because ended is a verb. Kai writes «I like pizza and I like chips.» That is two main clauses joined by and, but it's neater as «I like pizza and chips.» Mum in Liverpool says «our kid's» (her son's) sentences go on for ever: very long chains of clauses lose the reader, so mix short and long sentences.
fact: A clause is a group of words that contains a verb.
fact: A main clause makes sense on its own.
fact: A subordinate clause contains a verb but does not make sense on its own; it depends on a main clause.
fact: Subordinating conjunctions include because, when, if, although, while, after, before, until, since and as.
fact: Co-ordinating conjunctions such as and, but, or and so join two main clauses.
fact: When a subordinate clause comes before the main clause, a comma follows it.
fact: A phrase is a group of words without a verb, like «after the match» or «the big red bus».
fact: The same word can start a phrase or a clause: «after lunch» is a phrase, «after we had lunch» is a clause.
fact: A subordinate clause written alone, like «Because it was raining.», is an incomplete sentence.
fact: To find the main clause, cover each part and see which one still makes sense alone.
fact: Mixing short and long sentences makes writing clearer and more interesting.
fact: Slang: «wee» (Scotland, Northern Ireland) means little; «our kid» (Liverpool, Manchester) means my brother or sister; «no cap» means no lie, for real.
exercise: Jack says «When I got home» is not a full sentence. Why not? | It is a subordinate clause and needs a main clause | It has no verb, so it is only a phrase | It needs a question mark at the end | It is too short to be a sentence, which needs at least six words
exercise: Isla writes «Because it was raining.» on its own. What is the best fix? | We stayed in because it was raining. | Because it was raining and the clouds were very grey. | Because it was raining! | Because, it was raining.
exercise: «I ate my dinner although I wasn't hungry.» Underline the conjunction. | although | dinner | wasn't | hungry
exercise: «If you're quick, you'll catch the bus.» Which bit is the main clause? | you'll catch the bus | If you're quick | If | quick
exercise: «When it rained, we went inside.» Why is there a comma? | The subordinate clause comes first | Commas go after every fourth word | The sentence is a list of things | It shows that the speaker takes a breath in the middle
exercise: Kai writes «I like pizza and I like chips.» How can it be joined better? | I like pizza and chips. | I like pizza, I like chips. | I like pizza and I like chips and I like pizza. | I like, pizza and chips.
exercise: Mum says «our kid's sentences go on for ever». What does «our kid» mean? | My brother or sister | Our goat | Our neighbour's baby | Our teacher
exercise: Is «after the match» a clause or a phrase? | A phrase, it has no verb | A clause, because «after» is a conjunction | A clause, because «match» is a verb | A sentence
exercise: Jacob says «While I was gaming, Mum called.» Which is the subordinate clause? | While I was gaming | Mum called | Mum called me while | gaming
exercise: «Add a subordinate clause: The dog barked.» Which answer does it? | The dog barked because a stranger came. | The dog barked at the gate. | The dog barked loudly. | The big brown dog with the waggy tail barked.
exercise: What does a clause always contain? | A verb | A comma | A capital letter | A conjunction
exercise: Which word is a subordinating conjunction? | because | and | but | or
exercise: What does «no cap» mean? | No lie, for real | No hat | Not fair at all | No thanks
exercise: Which is a phrase, not a clause? | the old red bike | when the bell rang | because she smiled | if it snows
exercise: Which joins two main clauses? | and | although | because | if
test: «'Because it was raining.' is a sentence cos it's got a full stop». What's the best reply? | It's a subordinate clause: it needs a main clause | Yes, every group of words with a full stop is a sentence | It's a phrase, because it has no verb | It's a command, because «because» is bossy
test: ★★ «Although she was tired Amy finished the race.» Where does the comma go? | After «tired» | After «Although» | After «Amy» | It needs no comma
test: ★★ «after the match» is a phrase. Which change turns it into a clause? | after the match ended | after the long match | after the big match on Saturday | after the match, today
test: ★★★ «When the bell rang, the children who were waiting ran out.» How many clauses are there? | Three | One | Two | Four
test: ★★★ Which sentence has a subordinate clause at the END and so needs no comma? | We went out when the rain stopped. | When the rain stopped, we went out. | We went out, and the rain stopped. | The rain stopped. We went out.
test: ★★★ Swap the clauses round and punctuate: «You'll catch the bus if you're quick.» | If you're quick, you'll catch the bus. | If you're quick you'll, catch the bus. | You'll, if you're quick catch the bus. | If, you're quick, you'll catch the bus.
test: In a cartoon, a robot speaks only in subordinate clauses: «Because I am hungry. When the oil runs out.» Why does it sound strange? | Each one needs a main clause to make sense | Robots can't use verbs | Its sentences are too long | It uses far too many commas and full stops in every line
test: What would happen if a whole story was one sentence joined by «and then… and then…»? | It would be hard to follow | It would be easier to read | It would become a poem | It would have no verbs
test: «our kid says 'if' is just a word, not a conjunction». What's true? | If is a subordinating conjunction | If is a noun, the name of a word | If is a preposition, like under | If is only used in maths and coding, never in a sentence
test: «the wee lad ran home, his mam was waiting». Which fix is correct? | The wee lad ran home because his mam was waiting. | The wee lad ran home, his mam, was waiting. | The wee lad ran home, his mam was waiting at the door for him. | the wee lad ran home. his mam was waiting.
exam: Identify the main clause in the sentence «If you are quick, you will catch the bus». | you will catch the bus | If you are quick | If | quick, you will
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a subordinate clause? | A clause containing a verb which cannot stand alone as a sentence | A group of words containing no verb whatsoever | A clause which invariably ends in a question mark | A sentence consisting of one word only, such as a command addressed to a dog
exam: Select the response consistent with the established conventions concerning a subordinate clause placed before the main clause. | It is to be followed by a comma | It is to be followed by a full stop | It requires no punctuation | It must be enclosed in brackets
exam: Ascertain which of the following constitutes a phrase rather than a clause. | after the match | after the match ended | when the whistle blew | because the team lost
